WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.659
Reckford: It is great to be with you all today.

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:05.339
Wonderful to be back on campus.

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:07.609
I've been on your side of the podium.

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:10.800
I think it's been a long time since I've been in this room.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.910
Both when I was in your shoes a little bit excited, a little

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.490
bit anxious about

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.769
what the next steps might be as I got those coveted letters

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:20.079
MBA.

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:23.689
As Garth kindly pointed out, my education Stanford opened

00:00:23.709 --> 00:00:25.230
doors for me to have

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.410
opportunities at a number of major companies.

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:32.099
But I would also say, as I look back, the story is not as

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.369
neat as it sounds in

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.959
the resume and I've encountered, just as all of you will,

00:00:36.979 --> 00:00:37.979
bumps and bruises

00:00:37.979 --> 00:00:43.839
along the way, challenges that challenged my resolve and

00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:45.359
navigating those twist

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:48.879
and is turns that come along the way is a big part of our

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.489
collective challenge.

00:00:50.509 --> 00:00:53.259
I love my work with Habitat. I feel greatly privileged to

00:00:53.169 --> 00:00:54.669
get to be a part of

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.659
this mission and as I look back I can see how all the

00:00:57.679 --> 00:00:58.689
different experiences

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.989
along the way helped prepare me, even if I couldn't see it

00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:04.299
at the time.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:07.569
I certainly don't pretend to have all the answers as to what

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:07.939
make AS great

00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:12.150
leader but I can share a little bit of what I've observed.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.290
Your experiences and your choices are gonna determine your

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.760
individual journeys

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.280
but I hope you can benefit a little from what I've seen both

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:20.879
from the roles of

00:01:20.879 --> 00:01:24.129
some of the people I've had a chance to observe along the

00:01:23.129 --> 00:01:24.129
way.

00:01:24.129 --> 00:01:27.620
Like you, I've read books on leadership and collected my

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.640
share of quotes that

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.640
inspire me.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:34.189
Among those, a clip by leadership expert John Maxwell, who

00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:38.530
offers this tongue in

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:39.549
cheek encouragement.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:41.739
"When you're getting discouraged as a leader," said John, "

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:43.939
think of Mosas.

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:47.030
He led a million complaining people for 40 years in the

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:48.359
desert and never arrived

00:01:48.379 --> 00:01:51.120
where he was supposed to get.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.069
Mosas faced a lot of complaints, criticism, and just plain

00:01:55.089 --> 00:01:56.739
whining.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:01:59.719
Some days as a leader I can sympathize with Mosas.

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:03.739
I bet if he had to do it all over again make a note to self.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:06.670
Next time don't tell Pharaoh let all my people go."

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:06.890
&gt;&gt;

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:07.890
[Laughing]

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:11.009
Reckford: You know, leadership's hard and I think the more

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.030
people you attempt

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.149
to lead the harder it gets.

00:02:14.169 --> 00:02:16.589
And Mosas is a good example.

00:02:16.609 --> 00:02:19.609
As Garth laid the groundwork, I'm gonna share today in a

00:02:19.609 --> 00:02:20.609
context of my personal

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.209
faith and -- because my story doesn't really make sense

00:02:23.229 --> 00:02:25.959
without that but I hope,

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.989
regardless of your own faith perspectives, that the

00:02:28.009 --> 00:02:30.299
principles behind it will

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:33.139
resonate with you today.

00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:35.709
One of the first lessons -- and I doff around leadership --

00:02:35.729 --> 00:02:37.169
is a common sense

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:40.809
one, is the importance of role models.

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:44.659
I think all successful leaders identify either individuals

00:02:44.679 --> 00:02:45.679
or some composite of

00:02:45.679 --> 00:02:48.859
individuals that they wanna emulate and they may be really

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:49.879
great bosses,

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:52.509
teachers, counselors, parents.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.780
In my case I was blessed both with a mother who had a

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.620
ferocious sense of justice

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.370
and was very active in the civil rights movement and a

00:02:58.870 --> 00:02:59.870
rather formidable

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.969
grandmother, a woman named Millicent Fenwick, who was a

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:04.509
human rights pioneer, a

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.919
New Jersey congresswoman who encouraged me and my siblings

00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:11.259
at an early age to

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.539
think about what we were gonna do the serve the lost and

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.810
left out in the world.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.759
Her words to be useful.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.159
Walter Cronkite later called her the "Conscious of Congress.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:21.379
"

00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:24.629
I don't know how many of you have read the comic strip "

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:27.069
Doonesbury," the -- my

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.639
grandmother's often referred to for Garry Trudeau's

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:31.639
character Lacey Davenport in

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:36.109
the Doonesbury strips and she had a imposing regal presence

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:37.479
and prior to her

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.309
public career she had the unusual distinction of having

00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:41.329
written "The Vogue Book

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:44.479
of Etiquette," which sold a million copies in 1948.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.539
And so in order to graduate to the grownup table in grandma'

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.810
s house, you had to

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:51.359
be able to sit up straight, hold your fork properly, discuss

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:52.109
food problems in

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:55.889
sub Saharan Africa, which is both fascinating and terrifying

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:58.309
as a young child.

00:03:58.329 --> 00:04:01.699
And she was intimidating but was also spiritual at her core

00:04:01.719 --> 00:04:02.719
and almost every

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:06.439
time I would see her she would at some point drill in and

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:08.009
quote Micha six, six

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.499
through eight, from the Bible.

00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:14.509
"He has shown you, oh man, what is good and what does the

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.560
Lord require of you

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:21.500
but to love mercy, to walk humbly with your God?"

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:26.490
So act justly, love mercy, and walk humbly with your God.

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.810
Pretty good marching orders for most of us and that's become

00:04:29.829 --> 00:04:30.829
my life verse as

00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:33.579
well, though it took a long time to grow into that.

00:04:33.579 --> 00:04:36.670
I didn't come to Stanford right after college.

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:40.000
My original plan of going into -- going to law school then

00:04:40.019 --> 00:04:41.519
into politics got

00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:44.039
derailed when I discovered I had no actual interest in

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:45.290
practicing law so I had

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:49.060
to come up with plan B and talk my way into a job at Goldman

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.269
Sachs.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:53.490
Despite the latest images, this is a tough month for that

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:54.509
company.

00:04:54.509 --> 00:04:58.540
Uh, one of the most important lessons I learned early was

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.560
from one of the

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.310
co-leaders of Goldman at that time, a man named John

00:05:02.079 --> 00:05:04.180
Wineberg, and there were a

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:07.449
lot of less senior people in the firm who were caught up in

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:08.730
their own power and,

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:11.509
uh -- but John Wineberg took a personal interest in

00:05:11.529 --> 00:05:12.850
virtually everyone and he

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.980
had a deep, personal respect for everybody in the firm and

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.509
that's been something

00:05:18.529 --> 00:05:21.779
that's been critical to me to watch both for people I was

00:05:21.209 --> 00:05:22.209
gonna potentially work

00:05:22.209 --> 00:05:26.399
with or for, or also now when I'm hiring people to see, how

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:27.419
do they behave

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:30.199
towards the receptionist or the custodian, or people that

00:05:30.219 --> 00:05:32.160
don't have potential

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.139
positive influence for them as a sign of character.

00:05:36.159 --> 00:05:39.870
And not everyone will agree but to me a sort of core tenet

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:40.889
of leadership is that

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:45.230
fundamental respect for all people in organizations.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.750
I wasn't a great fit as a financial analyst.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:51.000
I was a poly sci English major with no accounting or finance

00:05:51.019 --> 00:05:52.589
preparation and had

00:05:52.609 --> 00:05:55.269
to suffer hard to live up to the idea.

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:58.079
I sold myself into the idea that I could learn finance

00:05:58.099 --> 00:05:59.099
faster than they could

00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:00.099
teach someone to communicate.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:02.099
I'm not sure it was totally true.

00:06:01.649 --> 00:06:01.849
&gt;&gt;

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:02.849
[Laughing]

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:05.579
Reckford: And I found it really intellectually interesting

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:06.599
but not very

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:10.110
emotionally satisfying and was living in New York City, in

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:11.540
Time Square and sort

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.949
of juxtaposition of walking by these huge numbers of

00:06:14.969 --> 00:06:15.969
homeless people in the

00:06:15.969 --> 00:06:18.980
morning and evening against the sort of big mergers and

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.000
acquisitions and

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.430
financing deals, uh, I think caused me to do some hard

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:25.449
thinking.

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:28.449
And I also had a growing sense of a disconnect between the

00:06:28.209 --> 00:06:29.209
life I'd imagined

00:06:29.209 --> 00:06:32.839
living and the way I was actually spending my time at that

00:06:32.859 --> 00:06:33.959
point.

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:36.729
And so felt -- I'd become very interested in business along

00:06:36.349 --> 00:06:37.939
the way but felt it

00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:40.959
needed to go off and have an adventure and was lucky enough

00:06:40.109 --> 00:06:41.129
to get the luge

00:06:41.149 --> 00:06:45.550
program and the -- no, to put the coaching in perspective,

00:06:45.569 --> 00:06:46.579
they only qualified

00:06:46.599 --> 00:06:49.099
because they were the host team and there were some

00:06:48.959 --> 00:06:49.980
parallels to the Jamaican

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.129
bobsled team but we did beat the Japanese.

00:06:53.149 --> 00:06:56.500
So for those of you who know Asia, that was significant.

00:06:56.519 --> 00:07:00.220
And it was a -- it was a huge life experience for me as an

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.240
emersion experience.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.990
I was living in the Korean training camp with all the

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:04.990
coaches and athletes and

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.560
the only other non Korean I got to see was a Russian boxing

00:07:08.579 --> 00:07:09.629
coach who was also

00:07:09.649 --> 00:07:11.290
in the dormitory.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:15.279
And it's sort of unfathomable to most of you a world with no

00:07:15.299 --> 00:07:16.299
internet, no e-mail

00:07:16.299 --> 00:07:18.199
and staggeringly expensive phone calls.

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:23.009
So really quite isolated from -- from all my relationships.

00:07:23.029 --> 00:07:26.529
And so it gave me a lot of time to read, and think and

00:07:25.609 --> 00:07:26.609
reflect.

00:07:26.609 --> 00:07:29.859
And the only westerner I saw on a regular basis was a man

00:07:29.859 --> 00:07:30.859
named Jim Peterson who

00:07:30.859 --> 00:07:33.609
was the other luge scholar that happened to be placed in

00:07:33.609 --> 00:07:34.859
Korea that year and he

00:07:34.859 --> 00:07:37.609
was a pastor and ethics professor teaching at one of the

00:07:37.109 --> 00:07:38.109
great Korean

00:07:38.109 --> 00:07:39.109
universities.

00:07:38.359 --> 00:07:41.359
And Jim was one of those rare people who had both the

00:07:41.359 --> 00:07:42.359
intellectual rigor to go

00:07:42.359 --> 00:07:45.359
as deep as I could go theologically and at the same time --

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:46.370
which really caught

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:50.050
my attention -- an inner peace and a quiet confidence that I

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.069
think really

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:54.620
contrasted I think to my sort of raw ambition and need to

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:56.689
impress people.

00:07:56.709 --> 00:08:00.209
And Jim took the time to get to know me and then made really

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:01.009
a quite

00:08:01.009 --> 00:08:02.399
extraordinary offer.

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:05.419
He offered me the -- all of his Monday nights for the rest

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:06.029
of that year and we

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:09.110
ended up spending three to five hours every Monday night

00:08:09.129 --> 00:08:10.550
walking throughout

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:13.750
Bible and doing systematic theology, so I had my private

00:08:13.769 --> 00:08:15.459
seminary.

00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:18.229
And I think for some people you all have your own

00:08:18.169 --> 00:08:19.419
experiences, faith can be sort

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:20.939
of a burning bush event.

00:08:20.959 --> 00:08:21.959
Less so for me.

00:08:21.959 --> 00:08:25.459
I had to have that integration of head and heart so it was a

00:08:25.449 --> 00:08:26.449
very gradual

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:30.730
process that eventually got me to -- S?ren Kierkegaard calls

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:32.649
that leap of faith

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.830
but it was finally -- became clear to me that the kind of

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.620
life I wanted to lead

00:08:37.639 --> 00:08:42.850
was one of faith and that was 1987 and I think for me hasn't

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:43.870
necessarily made

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:48.970
life easier but unquestionably made life better and richer.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.490
And so my time at Korea was coming to an end I applied to

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:53.009
business schools with

00:08:53.029 --> 00:08:55.610
the thought -- and Stanford was the cutting edge of this --

00:08:55.629 --> 00:08:57.070
that the skills I

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.659
could acquire in the business world were desperately needed

00:08:59.679 --> 00:09:00.679
in the non profit

00:09:00.679 --> 00:09:04.200
and service world as well and Stanford was very intriguing

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.220
to me because the

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.590
public management program was integrated into the regular

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.009
business curriculum.

00:09:10.029 --> 00:09:12.689
It -- while at Stanford, I got interested in business

00:09:12.709 --> 00:09:13.709
strategy and how

00:09:13.709 --> 00:09:15.840
organizations grow.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:20.480
And after graduation, again, you have to choose your job

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.309
criteria, my most

00:09:22.329 --> 00:09:25.079
important criteria was to get to Washington, D.C. where my

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:25.519
then girlfriend, now

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:26.519
wife, Ashley was.

00:09:26.409 --> 00:09:29.929
So I had a chance to work with Marriott working on new

00:09:29.949 --> 00:09:30.949
business development in

00:09:30.949 --> 00:09:34.629
their strategic planning group and had chance to -- was

00:09:34.649 --> 00:09:36.049
enjoying it very much --

00:09:36.069 --> 00:09:38.590
helped work on the launch of their senior living service,

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:39.860
their adult care

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:43.700
business, and got married, got promoted, life was good.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:48.139
And then we -- it pales in comparison, the S&amp;L crisis hit.

00:09:48.159 --> 00:09:52.500
So for those of you historians, it was not a great -- not as

00:09:52.519 --> 00:09:53.769
bad as now but was

00:09:53.709 --> 00:09:55.709
not a great time for the financial world.

00:09:55.529 --> 00:09:57.819
Marriott actually nearly went bankrupt and made significant

00:09:57.839 --> 00:09:58.839
cuts that included

00:09:58.839 --> 00:10:02.519
our strategic planning team and me and it was a little

00:10:02.539 --> 00:10:04.029
shocking to be laid off.

00:10:04.049 --> 00:10:07.669
I was newly married and I'd lost my job and even though it

00:10:07.689 --> 00:10:08.689
was sort of perfectly

00:10:08.689 --> 00:10:12.789
explicable, it was hard not to feel a little bit rejected.

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:14.909
And turned out fine.

00:10:14.929 --> 00:10:17.679
I was -- had the chance to start up a strategic planning

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:18.600
group for the real

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:21.350
estate arm of Disney which was a really fun and interesting

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.110
experience.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.620
Got to work on great projects and I think Disney taught me a

00:10:25.639 --> 00:10:26.799
lot about being a

00:10:26.819 --> 00:10:29.169
fanatic about great customer service which I really

00:10:29.189 --> 00:10:30.189
appreciate.

00:10:30.189 --> 00:10:32.939
But there was another side of Disney which I appreciate a

00:10:32.939 --> 00:10:34.389
little less and that

00:10:34.409 --> 00:10:38.490
was sort of the Burbank studio culture at the time and one

00:10:38.509 --> 00:10:39.759
of the former execs I

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:43.460
won't name was very well known and behaved for quotes like

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:44.909
this, "If you don't

00:10:44.929 --> 00:10:51.000
come in on Saturday, don't bother coming in on Sunday."

00:10:51.019 --> 00:10:52.019
Or --

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:52.220
&gt;&gt;

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:53.019
[Laughing]

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:54.029
Reckford: Or a little more frightening, though humorous, "

00:10:54.049 --> 00:10:55.449
other companies do

00:10:55.469 --> 00:10:56.590
good cop bad cop.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.459
We do bad cop Antichrist.

00:10:59.479 --> 00:10:59.679
."

00:10:59.679 --> 00:10:59.879
&gt;&gt;

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:00.679
[Laughing]

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:04.129
Reckford: And it was not good to apologize at the beginning

00:11:04.149 --> 00:11:05.149
of a negotiation to

00:11:05.149 --> 00:11:07.399
an outside party for the inherent unreasonableness of our

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.889
position.

00:11:08.909 --> 00:11:13.809
But it is, umm -- it leads to lesson No. 3 which is, you

00:11:13.829 --> 00:11:15.210
have to be very careful

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:18.500
about becoming a leader or working in a place that doesn't

00:11:18.519 --> 00:11:19.669
operate in sync with

00:11:19.689 --> 00:11:22.069
your personal values and that means -- obviously that starts

00:11:22.089 --> 00:11:23.089
with having to know

00:11:23.089 --> 00:11:27.699
your personal values and as I was sort of struggling with

00:11:27.719 --> 00:11:28.879
the implications of

00:11:28.899 --> 00:11:32.149
this ask sort of what kind of career I could have there, a

00:11:32.029 --> 00:11:33.029
headhunter called

00:11:33.029 --> 00:11:36.860
with an opportunity to come to Circuit City, and there was

00:11:36.879 --> 00:11:37.879
correlation but not

00:11:37.879 --> 00:11:40.629
causality that there was two companies I worked for don't

00:11:40.409 --> 00:11:41.409
exist anymore but --

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:41.610
&gt;&gt;

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:42.409
[Laughing?]

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:44.699
Reckford: Circuit City was on top of the world then.

00:11:44.719 --> 00:11:47.219
They'd been the fastest growing company on the stock

00:11:47.179 --> 00:11:49.389
exchange for the entire

00:11:49.409 --> 00:11:52.059
1980s and they had just started CarMax.

00:11:52.079 --> 00:11:53.579
You may not be too familiar.

00:11:53.579 --> 00:11:56.080
It was the -- it revolutionized the used car business.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.860
It was the first time anyone had ever offered warrantied,

00:11:58.879 --> 00:11:59.879
fixed price,

00:11:59.879 --> 00:12:03.159
returnable used cars and I thought that was really

00:12:03.179 --> 00:12:04.179
interesting.

00:12:04.179 --> 00:12:07.789
So we moved to Richmond, Virginia and I never intended to be

00:12:07.809 --> 00:12:09.240
in retail but found

00:12:09.259 --> 00:12:12.379
the work really interesting, learned a lot, but had a

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:14.939
growing -- had a growing

00:12:14.959 --> 00:12:17.819
desire to actually -- to run something at this point and I

00:12:17.839 --> 00:12:20.399
had lots of business

00:12:20.419 --> 00:12:21.899
development work.

00:12:21.919 --> 00:12:27.350
And so after four years, phone rang again and quite

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:28.370
unexpected and this was

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.610
Musicland which is about a 2 million-dollar retailer at the

00:12:31.629 --> 00:12:33.269
time, back when

00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:36.289
people actually bought music and movies on plastic discs,

00:12:36.309 --> 00:12:37.309
which is another thing

00:12:37.309 --> 00:12:40.769
that's hard to believe, and we were the leading mall

00:12:40.789 --> 00:12:42.049
retailer of entertainment

00:12:42.069 --> 00:12:46.659
products and it is a disappearing category.

00:12:46.679 --> 00:12:49.569
And Napster had just launched so they could see the future

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:50.589
and offered me a

00:12:50.589 --> 00:12:54.090
chance to run the operations and help navigate a -- a new

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:56.250
path for the company.

00:12:56.269 --> 00:12:58.519
I can't say my wife was terribly enthused.

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:01.089
She couldn't really understand why God would want a nice

00:13:00.679 --> 00:13:02.079
southern lady to move

00:13:02.099 --> 00:13:06.799
to Minneapolis, the land of endless winter and, uh -- but I

00:13:06.819 --> 00:13:08.009
had a very strong

00:13:08.029 --> 00:13:10.779
sense of what we were supposed to do and she -- she

00:13:10.689 --> 00:13:11.939
grudgingly agreed so we made

00:13:11.879 --> 00:13:12.879
the move.

00:13:12.879 --> 00:13:16.669
We actually had a couple of record years and yet one of the

00:13:16.689 --> 00:13:17.689
more discouraging

00:13:17.689 --> 00:13:20.439
things from a business perspective, it was very hard to see

00:13:20.189 --> 00:13:21.189
how the company

00:13:21.189 --> 00:13:23.689
could survive not just against the brick and mortar but

00:13:23.639 --> 00:13:25.720
against the inevitable

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:28.569
digital transformation that was coming.

00:13:28.589 --> 00:13:31.960
So we ended up selling the company to Best Buy which was a

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:32.980
great win for our

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:36.079
shareholders and a great lost really for our -- ultimately

00:13:36.099 --> 00:13:37.349
for our staff and for

00:13:36.409 --> 00:13:37.409
Best Buy.

00:13:37.409 --> 00:13:40.120
It was not a great acquisition.

00:13:40.139 --> 00:13:43.750
And I stayed for a year to help try to make the acquisition

00:13:43.769 --> 00:13:45.149
work but also then

00:13:45.169 --> 00:13:49.069
became clear that -- that I was redundant with the --

00:13:49.089 --> 00:13:50.360
appropriately put in Best

00:13:50.379 --> 00:13:52.179
Buy head of the division.

00:13:52.199 --> 00:13:56.079
And so Best Buy was kind enough to offer different

00:13:56.099 --> 00:13:57.799
opportunities but I had a

00:13:57.819 --> 00:14:01.279
growing sense that it was time -- it had taken much longer

00:14:01.299 --> 00:14:02.299
than I has expected

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:05.549
and maybe this is the time to begin the transition that I'd

00:14:05.049 --> 00:14:06.049
always planned of

00:14:06.049 --> 00:14:07.049
the nonprofit world.

00:14:07.049 --> 00:14:10.220
And I'd been increasingly passionate about some of the -- my

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:11.500
volunteer work,

00:14:11.519 --> 00:14:14.199
which had been helping coach pastors and priests in the

00:14:14.219 --> 00:14:15.219
leadership side of

00:14:15.219 --> 00:14:16.389
churches.

00:14:16.409 --> 00:14:19.159
And so even though I regularly counsel people, don't leave

00:14:18.899 --> 00:14:19.899
without knowing what

00:14:19.899 --> 00:14:24.090
your next step is in this case made a decision to jump and

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:25.110
took off with some

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:29.100
big ideas and a really ferocious non-compete clause but I

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:31.860
did have a walk away

00:14:31.879 --> 00:14:34.559
package so it was not a particularly brave move.

00:14:34.579 --> 00:14:38.709
For us, the freedom meant that I was gonna have a lot of

00:14:38.729 --> 00:14:39.729
time with, at that

00:14:39.729 --> 00:14:42.689
point, our very young children at home with Ashley and time

00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:43.709
to have a little

00:14:43.709 --> 00:14:45.959
enough space to really think about the next step.

00:14:45.959 --> 00:14:50.129
A little while after that Ashley had encouraged me to do

00:14:50.149 --> 00:14:51.149
something I had never

00:14:51.149 --> 00:14:54.649
really had time to do and always wanted to do which is to go

00:14:54.399 --> 00:14:55.399
on an international

00:14:55.399 --> 00:14:58.649
mission trip and I know many of you have done this now -- or

00:14:58.399 --> 00:14:59.399
service trip. I

00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:02.149
wanted to go to Uganda, where our church had been sponsoring

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:02.899
all the AIDS

00:15:02.899 --> 00:15:05.449
orphans in the Rakai district where AIDS really broke out.

00:15:05.469 --> 00:15:08.969
But I was too late to join that trip and ended up, through a

00:15:08.529 --> 00:15:10.059
series of events,

00:15:10.079 --> 00:15:12.829
being able to join an opportunity to serve with the Dalats

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:16.129
in central India.

00:15:16.149 --> 00:15:19.329
And the Bungi -- the Dalats are commonly known as the

00:15:19.349 --> 00:15:20.349
untouchables or the

00:15:20.349 --> 00:15:23.069
traditional outcasts in Indian community and the Bungi are

00:15:23.089 --> 00:15:24.339
sort of the bottom of

00:15:24.339 --> 00:15:27.509
the bottom of the social pecking order, literally only aloud

00:15:27.529 --> 00:15:28.929
to clean latrines

00:15:28.949 --> 00:15:31.949
and clean up after dead animals and living in conditions

00:15:31.969 --> 00:15:32.969
that really

00:15:32.969 --> 00:15:36.429
unimaginably bad and about half the kids without some kind

00:15:36.449 --> 00:15:37.500
of intervention are

00:15:37.519 --> 00:15:41.009
dying by the age of 13 from a combination of poor nutrition

00:15:41.029 --> 00:15:44.139
and poor health.

00:15:44.159 --> 00:15:48.000
And I think for me, uh, just shattered me to see people

00:15:48.019 --> 00:15:49.019
living in these

00:15:49.019 --> 00:15:54.199
conditions and it was -- I don't know that I knew but I

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:55.219
essentially after a

00:15:55.219 --> 00:15:57.969
couple weeks there came back with a pretty strong sense that

00:15:57.919 --> 00:15:58.919
I was -- it was

00:15:58.919 --> 00:16:01.919
time to spend the rest of my time doing something to help

00:16:01.259 --> 00:16:02.559
the poor.

00:16:02.579 --> 00:16:06.189
And I think for me it reignited some of those early passions

00:16:06.209 --> 00:16:07.209
around social

00:16:07.209 --> 00:16:10.329
justice that had softened down a little bit over the years.

00:16:10.349 --> 00:16:13.339
It was what Bill Hybels, pastor of the Willow Creek Church,

00:16:13.359 --> 00:16:14.359
calls a holy

00:16:14.359 --> 00:16:15.359
discontent.

00:16:14.609 --> 00:16:17.539
That was a nicely-termed phrase.

00:16:17.559 --> 00:16:20.399
And we have plenty of discontent in our society.

00:16:20.419 --> 00:16:22.169
We see it all over the place.

00:16:22.169 --> 00:16:24.419
People are watching some terrible event unfold in their

00:16:24.419 --> 00:16:25.419
community of the world

00:16:25.419 --> 00:16:27.169
and the response is, that's terrible.

00:16:26.919 --> 00:16:29.559
Somebody ought to do something about that and they change

00:16:29.579 --> 00:16:30.970
channels, watch

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.689
"American Idol".

00:16:32.709 --> 00:16:37.149
And a holy discontent and contrast is when you or I see

00:16:37.169 --> 00:16:38.289
those terrible events

00:16:38.309 --> 00:16:41.309
unfold in our community of the world and the response is, I

00:16:41.079 --> 00:16:42.409
can't stand that and

00:16:42.429 --> 00:16:44.179
I'm gonna do something about it.

00:16:43.959 --> 00:16:47.459
You turn off the TV, and get off the couch and get into the

00:16:47.099 --> 00:16:48.099
community and decide

00:16:48.099 --> 00:16:51.099
to be making -- try to make the world a little bit better.

00:16:51.099 --> 00:16:57.389
It's -- so I came back with a new passion but not a very

00:16:57.409 --> 00:16:59.000
clear direction.

00:16:59.019 --> 00:17:02.269
And what was -- took me back for the first time really in my

00:17:02.049 --> 00:17:03.049
whole life suddenly

00:17:03.049 --> 00:17:04.500
all the doors are closing.

00:17:04.519 --> 00:17:07.769
And now I can look back and see none of those were really

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:08.099
the right things but

00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:10.869
got to the finals of a couple of different really exciting

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:11.569
nonprofit jobs and

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.490
didn't get them.

00:17:13.509 --> 00:17:16.450
And I think we MBAs are particularly at risk of defining

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.589
ourselves what we do.

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.799
So when people would ask the inevitable question, I had a --

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:24.160
sort of a matter of

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.879
discipline to bite my tongue and resist the urge to say, "I

00:17:27.899 --> 00:17:30.009
used to be XYZ" or

00:17:30.029 --> 00:17:33.529
"I used do XYZ" and just say that, you know, I was home with

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:34.490
my family and

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:37.549
trying to figure out what I was supposed to do next.

00:17:37.569 --> 00:17:40.569
And I'm not a particularly patient person so I didn't --

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:41.490
waiting isn't very easy

00:17:41.509 --> 00:17:46.460
for me and I think when you wait and question it can drive

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:47.730
you a little bit nuts

00:17:47.599 --> 00:17:50.119
for us type A types.

00:17:50.139 --> 00:17:53.579
And when I first left Best Buy turned down some very good

00:17:53.599 --> 00:17:54.599
business jobs, then I

00:17:54.599 --> 00:17:58.049
started wondering, did I get the signals wrong?

00:17:58.069 --> 00:18:00.519
Did I miss -- miss the opportunity?

00:18:00.539 --> 00:18:03.899
And in the meantime I was cranking way up on my volunteer

00:18:03.919 --> 00:18:05.450
time both with our

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.730
kids at their school and at church and quite unexpectedly

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:11.119
the -- all the

00:18:11.139 --> 00:18:13.970
leadership development work I was doing at our very large

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.289
church ended up with

00:18:16.309 --> 00:18:20.099
the church asking me to run the church as executive pastor,

00:18:20.119 --> 00:18:21.119
to free up the

00:18:21.119 --> 00:18:24.079
pastor to just preach and teach.

00:18:24.099 --> 00:18:27.599
And it was not what I was looking to do and most of my

00:18:27.599 --> 00:18:28.599
friends thought this was

00:18:28.599 --> 00:18:31.849
a really bad career move, and would not be a smart thing to

00:18:31.099 --> 00:18:32.099
do.

00:18:32.099 --> 00:18:35.349
But as best we could tell -- best I could tell, it was what

00:18:35.349 --> 00:18:36.349
I was supposed to

00:18:36.349 --> 00:18:37.349
do.

00:18:36.599 --> 00:18:40.250
So went into full-time church work and in fact loved it and

00:18:40.269 --> 00:18:41.519
it was a great time

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.569
for our family and it was -- it was a very fulfilling time

00:18:44.589 --> 00:18:46.089
for me and I was not

00:18:45.399 --> 00:18:48.369
actually looking when probably the most important phone call

00:18:48.389 --> 00:18:49.809
I've gotten came in

00:18:49.829 --> 00:18:53.259
and it was from a partner at Spencer Stuart, who had

00:18:53.279 --> 00:18:55.180
recruited me in the past,

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.950
who asked, Jonathan, do you know anybody who'd be interested

00:18:57.779 --> 00:18:58.779
in CEO for Habitat

00:18:58.789 --> 00:19:00.849
for Humanity?

00:19:00.869 --> 00:19:04.859
And, uh, a chill went down my spine and I asked a little bit

00:19:04.879 --> 00:19:05.879
shyly, "Does it

00:19:05.879 --> 00:19:08.769
have to be somebody famous?"

00:19:08.789 --> 00:19:11.619
And, uh, and then went home and wrote a very passionate

00:19:11.639 --> 00:19:12.639
letter about why this

00:19:12.639 --> 00:19:15.990
was the kind of thing I had been preparing for for the last

00:19:16.009 --> 00:19:17.509
many years.

00:19:17.529 --> 00:19:20.029
And I felt like finally here was something that really

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:20.899
combined all of my

00:19:20.919 --> 00:19:23.460
passions and put everything together.

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:28.670
It was, for me, very much my dream job and I really wanted

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:29.690
it very badly, did

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:34.180
not expect to get it, and was very surprised and delighted

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:35.200
to -- to have the

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:36.200
chance.

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:40.159
And I -- that's a lot of detail but I wanted -- I share that

00:19:40.179 --> 00:19:41.179
because the fourth

00:19:41.179 --> 00:19:45.339
lesson I would share is, you know, my dad was a teacher and

00:19:45.359 --> 00:19:46.359
he taught one place

00:19:46.359 --> 00:19:47.859
for 45 years and then retired.

00:19:47.859 --> 00:19:51.129
I think that world is changed, I think in the business world

00:19:51.149 --> 00:19:52.149
especially, careers

00:19:52.149 --> 00:19:54.649
are enormously more fluid than they used to be.

00:19:54.669 --> 00:19:58.250
And -- but I wanna encourage you is to -- is to not fight

00:19:58.269 --> 00:19:59.359
the detours along the

00:19:59.379 --> 00:20:01.879
way, and particularly to take advantage of learning from the

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:02.730
white spaces in

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.500
your -- in your career and the failures which I often found

00:20:05.519 --> 00:20:06.519
were the richest

00:20:06.519 --> 00:20:09.519
learning times for me.

00:20:09.539 --> 00:20:13.220
I arrived at a really critical inflection point for Habitat

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:14.240
in many ways.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:17.940
Like very successful startup companies, they were in the

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:18.960
process of

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.730
transitioning from a highly charismatic entrepreneurial

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.089
founder and had grown

00:20:24.109 --> 00:20:26.859
far bigger than they'd ever imagined and needed, in the

00:20:26.609 --> 00:20:29.269
board's view, more

00:20:29.289 --> 00:20:32.009
professional management to build out a sort of

00:20:32.029 --> 00:20:33.029
infrastructure and leadership

00:20:33.049 --> 00:20:35.879
team to support this exponential growth that they'd had

00:20:35.899 --> 00:20:38.609
around the world.

00:20:38.629 --> 00:20:42.170
Habitat needed to become more sufficient, more sustainable,

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:43.569
and more equipped to

00:20:43.589 --> 00:20:46.579
be able to continue to sustain the growth given how much --

00:20:46.599 --> 00:20:47.849
the side of the need

00:20:47.559 --> 00:20:49.559
in virtually every country in which we serve.

00:20:49.299 --> 00:20:52.579
I think many of you -- I presume all everyone's familiar

00:20:52.599 --> 00:20:54.339
with Habitat but let me

00:20:54.359 --> 00:20:57.289
give you just a quick Habitat 101 class.

00:20:57.309 --> 00:21:00.589
Most importantly, we build houses in partnership with -- not

00:21:00.609 --> 00:21:02.019
for -- families who

00:21:02.039 --> 00:21:05.420
need decent and affordable homes in the U.S. and across the

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:06.440
world.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.190
We don't give the houses away.

00:21:08.139 --> 00:21:10.670
Homeowners make a down payment and purchase their homes from

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:11.940
us on a no profit

00:21:11.549 --> 00:21:13.000
basis.

00:21:13.019 --> 00:21:15.690
Homeowners are selected based on three core criteria.

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:19.099
First, need, they wouldn't be otherwise able to obtain a

00:21:19.119 --> 00:21:20.920
home loan or mortgage;

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.339
second, the ability to re-pay a very affordable no-profit

00:21:24.359 --> 00:21:25.609
mortgage over a 30-to

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.950
50-year period; and three, the willingness to partner with

00:21:27.879 --> 00:21:28.879
Habitat.

00:21:28.879 --> 00:21:31.629
And for us, partner means is willingness to put hundreds of

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:32.210
hours of sweat

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:34.869
equity into building their home and helping build other

00:21:34.889 --> 00:21:36.139
people's homes, and also

00:21:35.829 --> 00:21:38.079
to go through financial and home management training in

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:38.990
preparation for

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:40.909
homeownership.

00:21:40.929 --> 00:21:43.809
In the same way, then, their mortgage payments revolve in

00:21:43.829 --> 00:21:45.230
the community so in

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.809
addition to recipients, they become donors to help other

00:21:47.829 --> 00:21:48.829
families have a chance

00:21:48.829 --> 00:21:51.129
at homeownership.

00:21:51.149 --> 00:21:53.649
As a Christian ministry now working into our fourth decade,

00:21:53.349 --> 00:21:54.599
we've got a pretty

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:57.470
good idea what to expect when a family gets, for the first

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:58.460
time, a decent place

00:21:58.460 --> 00:21:59.460
to live.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:02.519
We see huge impacts on health, the children go up -- growing

00:22:02.539 --> 00:22:04.109
up in decent homes

00:22:04.129 --> 00:22:06.569
have much better health outcomes; healthy children have much

00:22:06.589 --> 00:22:07.589
better educational

00:22:07.589 --> 00:22:11.309
outcomes; and in addition to the sort of physical and

00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:12.329
material benefits of

00:22:12.339 --> 00:22:15.230
homeownership, there's a huge psychological change that

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:16.829
happens as well when a

00:22:16.849 --> 00:22:18.599
people have a place to call home.

00:22:18.599 --> 00:22:21.599
And what we've seen over and over again is family members

00:22:21.189 --> 00:22:22.439
are so proud of their

00:22:22.439 --> 00:22:24.689
new houses their homes become social centers, they become

00:22:24.189 --> 00:22:25.189
anchors in the

00:22:25.189 --> 00:22:27.980
community to stabilize communities, and many communities

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.000
have been lifted from

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:31.819
forgotten blighted areas to areas that have been restored

00:22:31.839 --> 00:22:32.839
and become inviting

00:22:32.839 --> 00:22:34.589
areas.

00:22:34.609 --> 00:22:38.200
In fact now with the housing crisis, purchasing up these

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:39.399
empty, foreclosed homes

00:22:39.419 --> 00:22:41.169
has become a high priority for us.

00:22:41.019 --> 00:22:43.769
So we're shifting our mix more away from new home

00:22:43.519 --> 00:22:45.029
construction towards rehabbing

00:22:45.049 --> 00:22:48.789
empty homes and in that and around those rehabbed homes

00:22:48.809 --> 00:22:49.809
tearing down the worst

00:22:49.809 --> 00:22:52.079
ones, building targeted new ones, and then doing

00:22:52.099 --> 00:22:54.359
weatherization and repairs to

00:22:54.379 --> 00:22:57.399
preserve homeownership, especially for the elderly and those

00:22:57.419 --> 00:22:58.419
communities in that

00:22:58.419 --> 00:23:01.169
trying to do as much as we can to revitalize whole

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:02.700
communities versus individual

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:04.180
homes.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:05.769
That's a Habitat model.

00:23:05.789 --> 00:23:09.039
It's a model that's been effective now for well over 30

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:10.250
years.

00:23:10.269 --> 00:23:13.819
As Garth said, last year we helped over 61,000 families

00:23:13.839 --> 00:23:15.089
through a mix of new

00:23:14.769 --> 00:23:18.369
homes and rehabs and major repairs, which was at almost

00:23:18.389 --> 00:23:19.389
triple where we were

00:23:19.389 --> 00:23:21.319
five years ago.

00:23:21.339 --> 00:23:24.680
The nonprofit world is at least as complex from a management

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:25.700
perspective as

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:28.059
anything I found in the business world.

00:23:28.079 --> 00:23:31.490
We have over 1500 affiliates in the U.S. which goes back to

00:23:31.509 --> 00:23:33.079
that Mosas quote.

00:23:33.099 --> 00:23:36.349
I think the -- I once had a reporter from the New York Times

00:23:36.349 --> 00:23:37.349
say this big change

00:23:37.349 --> 00:23:40.099
we're putting in had caused 12 affiliates to get upset.

00:23:39.849 --> 00:23:40.940
And I just laughed.

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.960
I thought, if there was anything we ever did that only 12

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:44.500
affiliates were upset

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:46.750
at we would be doing Hallelujahs.

00:23:46.769 --> 00:23:49.519
But when you add that together, we're actually the largest

00:23:48.909 --> 00:23:49.909
home builder in the

00:23:49.909 --> 00:23:51.509
United States.

00:23:51.529 --> 00:23:53.220
We serve in over 80 countries.

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:56.240
If you pulled all the Habitat budgets together it's about a

00:23:55.799 --> 00:23:57.559
billion and a half

00:23:57.579 --> 00:24:01.399
in terms of total revenue and we increasingly are able to

00:24:01.419 --> 00:24:02.419
play a significant

00:24:02.419 --> 00:24:05.349
role in terms of shaping housing policies around the world

00:24:05.369 --> 00:24:07.159
as well.

00:24:07.179 --> 00:24:10.109
Stanford stood for the idea, although I look back to Ernie

00:24:10.129 --> 00:24:11.129
Arbuckle that the

00:24:11.129 --> 00:24:13.879
same skills that will make you successful in the for-profit

00:24:13.629 --> 00:24:14.629
world are needed in

00:24:14.629 --> 00:24:15.919
the nonprofit world.

00:24:15.939 --> 00:24:19.930
And I took that seriously but I can tell you, it is more

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.200
true than ever now and

00:24:21.069 --> 00:24:24.869
I think it's a much more widely accepted idea now as well.

00:24:24.889 --> 00:24:27.639
I know certainly we're being called to use those skills.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.490
We've set a new goal now at Habitat to serve a hundred

00:24:29.939 --> 00:24:30.939
thousand families per

00:24:30.939 --> 00:24:35.480
year by 2013 which is gonna challenge us to find new ways to

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:36.619
scale up and

00:24:36.639 --> 00:24:40.450
increase our impact in the communities in which we serve.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:43.470
And I feel very blessed to have a small role in that

00:24:42.629 --> 00:24:43.629
process.

00:24:43.629 --> 00:24:48.500
I have the -- the sad part of my job is I continue to travel

00:24:48.519 --> 00:24:49.519
across the world to

00:24:49.519 --> 00:24:52.269
places and see the way the families live all across the

00:24:52.169 --> 00:24:53.659
world and it is truly

00:24:53.679 --> 00:24:57.389
heartbreaking still and you know the numbers.

00:24:57.409 --> 00:25:01.399
But I've also had the chance to see what just as we call a

00:25:01.419 --> 00:25:02.669
hand up, not a hand

00:25:02.599 --> 00:25:06.619
out, can do to let a family lift themselves out of poverty

00:25:06.639 --> 00:25:07.639
and the fundamental

00:25:07.639 --> 00:25:09.899
difference that can make in life.

00:25:09.919 --> 00:25:13.429
Last year I looked into the eyes of a six- girl in a small

00:25:13.449 --> 00:25:14.700
town in El Salvador

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.970
who told me her dreams of being a lawyer so she could help

00:25:17.299 --> 00:25:18.299
others.

00:25:18.299 --> 00:25:20.369
Her mother was already living her dream.

00:25:20.389 --> 00:25:23.389
She'd always wanted to be a hairdresser and have her own

00:25:22.589 --> 00:25:23.839
salon but had never had

00:25:23.579 --> 00:25:24.579
the chance.

00:25:24.579 --> 00:25:29.659
And now she'd opened a salon in her new Habitat home as soon

00:25:29.679 --> 00:25:30.679
as the girls who

00:25:30.679 --> 00:25:33.759
can now go to school went off, she started treating

00:25:33.779 --> 00:25:35.279
customers and she's able to

00:25:35.149 --> 00:25:38.799
earn an income and spend the time with her girls.

00:25:38.819 --> 00:25:43.190
In Vietnam last fall I met a family 51-year-old grandfather

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:44.210
who had literally

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:47.539
spent his entirely living on a boat with his extended

00:25:47.559 --> 00:25:48.629
family.

00:25:48.649 --> 00:25:51.639
We have the privilege of tucking our children into bed.

00:25:51.659 --> 00:25:54.659
They would tie the children in at night because they'd lost

00:25:54.309 --> 00:25:55.309
one grandchild to

00:25:55.309 --> 00:25:57.889
drowning in bad weather.

00:25:57.909 --> 00:26:00.909
And if you could see the boat, uh, or the boats these

00:26:00.369 --> 00:26:01.599
families were living in,

00:26:01.619 --> 00:26:04.619
it would just -- it would just break your heart.

00:26:04.639 --> 00:26:08.569
And I had the privilege of dedicating with them -- we talk

00:26:08.589 --> 00:26:10.039
about a house being

00:26:10.059 --> 00:26:11.940
the foundation for a decent life.

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:14.710
This family, for the first time ever, was gonna have a

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.710
foundation and live on

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.480
dry land which meant their kids can now go to school,

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:19.470
unbelievable health

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:22.929
impacts for -- for them and for their future.

00:26:22.949 --> 00:26:25.449
So my days don't get much better than that.

00:26:25.329 --> 00:26:27.700
And a quick review.

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:31.089
The leaders I've respected the most have emulated good role

00:26:31.109 --> 00:26:32.109
models and picked

00:26:32.109 --> 00:26:36.129
the right ones; they respect and value all of their staff

00:26:36.149 --> 00:26:37.720
and teams; they find a

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:41.399
place that -- to work that is in sync with their defined

00:26:41.419 --> 00:26:43.389
personal values; and

00:26:43.409 --> 00:26:46.049
they remain adaptable and as eager to learn from their

00:26:46.069 --> 00:26:47.069
failures as their

00:26:47.069 --> 00:26:48.069
successes.

00:26:47.319 --> 00:26:52.809
I think for me, when I look back on the story, my life

00:26:52.829 --> 00:26:53.829
changed dramatically for

00:26:53.829 --> 00:26:58.019
the better during one of the sort of quiet times in life

00:26:58.039 --> 00:26:59.539
when I wasn't earning a

00:26:59.289 --> 00:27:02.289
dime and probably was the most frustrated and I don't think

00:27:02.049 --> 00:27:03.049
I could have ever

00:27:03.049 --> 00:27:06.089
charted the path the led to Habitat.

00:27:06.109 --> 00:27:09.609
If I -- I wouldn't have been ready for this role if I had

00:27:09.099 --> 00:27:10.099
come straight from

00:27:10.099 --> 00:27:14.109
Best Buy and I also know if I had taken the job at the

00:27:14.129 --> 00:27:15.379
church just so I could

00:27:15.379 --> 00:27:18.379
get a job like Habitat, I'm fully convinced that never would

00:27:18.099 --> 00:27:19.099
have worked.

00:27:19.099 --> 00:27:23.629
It is -- in my experience, the times that I really loved

00:27:23.649 --> 00:27:24.899
work were was when I

00:27:24.829 --> 00:27:27.139
was really focused on getting projects done or doing

00:27:27.159 --> 00:27:29.789
something that mattered and

00:27:29.809 --> 00:27:32.690
the least successful were those when I was focused on trying

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:33.710
to grow my career.

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:37.210
And my hope for all of you is that you'll find the same

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:38.059
satisfaction. I hope

00:27:38.079 --> 00:27:41.519
and pray that each of you will find a vocation that allows

00:27:41.539 --> 00:27:42.539
you to take your

00:27:42.549 --> 00:27:45.409
gifts and skills and passion and do something, whether in

00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:46.429
the private sector or

00:27:46.429 --> 00:27:48.929
the nonprofit world, that makes the world a little bit

00:27:48.329 --> 00:27:49.329
better.

00:27:49.329 --> 00:27:55.289
I think we all get bombarded constantly and you all are and

00:27:55.309 --> 00:27:56.450
will continue to be

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.750
bombarded with definitions of the good life that base

00:27:58.769 --> 00:27:59.769
everything based on the

00:27:59.769 --> 00:28:03.019
size of your salary and the house you live in, the car you

00:28:03.019 --> 00:28:04.019
drive, how much

00:28:04.019 --> 00:28:07.599
plastic surgery you've had, how perfect you look.

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:10.779
And I think it brings me to the final lesson.

00:28:10.799 --> 00:28:14.490
When, as you get increasing responsibilities in leadership,

00:28:14.509 --> 00:28:15.759
I think you'll be

00:28:15.449 --> 00:28:19.329
awash in temptations and I think you gotta be very

00:28:19.349 --> 00:28:21.079
intentional in defining what

00:28:21.099 --> 00:28:26.009
a good life is for you and be -- in pursuing it because the

00:28:26.029 --> 00:28:27.029
lure of letting

00:28:27.029 --> 00:28:30.859
other people define that for you is very tempting.

00:28:30.879 --> 00:28:34.379
And it's very easy for all of us to get our egos and

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:35.029
identities tied up with our

00:28:35.049 --> 00:28:39.240
jobs or mixed up with our jobs and I find I can be more

00:28:39.259 --> 00:28:40.259
careful than ever now

00:28:40.259 --> 00:28:44.179
that I'm in a comparatively public position.

00:28:44.199 --> 00:28:47.699
As a son of a classics professor, I think we all need to be

00:28:47.399 --> 00:28:48.399
careful of the idea

00:28:48.399 --> 00:28:51.149
of hubris, that idea of putting ourselves on par with the

00:28:51.149 --> 00:28:52.149
gods.

00:28:52.169 --> 00:28:55.419
When I see the things that blow leaders up, and usually it's

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:55.970
around money, sex,

00:28:55.970 --> 00:29:01.669
and power, they're really all wrapped up in pride and you

00:29:01.689 --> 00:29:02.939
have to be aware of

00:29:02.939 --> 00:29:05.689
those temptations and I think the ones who say, that could

00:29:04.559 --> 00:29:05.559
never happen to me,

00:29:05.559 --> 00:29:08.649
that's often the first step down that path.

00:29:08.669 --> 00:29:11.210
And I've seen some dear friends and otherwise really

00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:12.950
effective and great leaders

00:29:12.970 --> 00:29:16.009
totally derail not only their careers but their families

00:29:16.029 --> 00:29:17.519
because they weren't

00:29:17.539 --> 00:29:20.289
aware of those temptations or didn't think it could happen

00:29:19.809 --> 00:29:20.809
to them.

00:29:20.809 --> 00:29:24.029
In my experience it's better to be humble than to be

00:29:24.049 --> 00:29:26.189
humbled.

00:29:26.209 --> 00:29:29.459
And I don't think you have to think of humility as a

00:29:28.889 --> 00:29:29.889
limitation. Jim Collins,

00:29:29.889 --> 00:29:33.129
who was a GSB entrepreneur ship professor back when we were

00:29:33.149 --> 00:29:34.149
here before he

00:29:34.149 --> 00:29:37.980
became a guru, discovered the idea of a level five leader in

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:39.000
his research and I

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.480
was so pleased to see that and that idea of people that had,

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:43.750
as he would say, a

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:46.529
paradoxical combination of personal humility and fierce

00:29:46.549 --> 00:29:47.549
ambition for their

00:29:47.549 --> 00:29:49.329
mission or cause.

00:29:49.349 --> 00:29:53.299
Not that level five leaders have no ego or self-interest.

00:29:53.319 --> 00:29:55.960
In fact, they're incredibly ambitious but it's their

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.599
ambition for what they're

00:29:57.619 --> 00:30:01.579
trying to get done rather than for themselves.

00:30:01.599 --> 00:30:05.659
And tied to that is integrity, which we just see over and

00:30:05.679 --> 00:30:06.679
over again can be

00:30:06.679 --> 00:30:08.730
destroyed in an instant.

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:11.970
Warren Bennis, another leadership guru, describes

00:30:11.990 --> 00:30:13.470
effectively leadership as a

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:17.339
carefully-balanced three-pronged stool of ambition,

00:30:17.359 --> 00:30:19.730
confidence, and a moral

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:21.359
compass.

00:30:21.379 --> 00:30:24.460
And sadly, we can't watch the businesses these days without

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:25.480
seeing what happens

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:28.099
when you have destructive achievers, high ambition, high

00:30:28.119 --> 00:30:29.349
competence, and no

00:30:29.369 --> 00:30:30.369
moral compass.

00:30:29.869 --> 00:30:36.669
And, uh, leadership ultimately comes -- starts or emanates

00:30:36.689 --> 00:30:37.689
from character and

00:30:37.689 --> 00:30:40.559
character comes from a Greek word that literally means a

00:30:40.579 --> 00:30:42.710
tool that marks or

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.109
engraves us.

00:30:44.129 --> 00:30:47.480
And each day, each choice, each action we make we're in the

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:48.500
constant process of

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:51.230
either making or unmaking our character either moving more

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:52.250
towards the kinda

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:55.509
person we wanna be or moving back and it's ultimately how

00:30:55.529 --> 00:30:56.669
our colleagues and

00:30:56.689 --> 00:30:57.939
others are gonna measure us.

00:30:57.909 --> 00:31:01.799
So you have to choose those values you're gonna live by,

00:31:01.819 --> 00:31:03.069
you've gotta choose the

00:31:02.819 --> 00:31:06.019
lines that you're not willing to cross before those

00:31:06.039 --> 00:31:07.179
situations actually come

00:31:07.199 --> 00:31:08.289
along.

00:31:08.309 --> 00:31:12.069
And, uh, and finally, you gotta -- you've gotta lead from

00:31:12.089 --> 00:31:13.679
passion, not just from

00:31:13.699 --> 00:31:14.699
position.

00:31:14.679 --> 00:31:17.269
And I hope -- I didn't always know.

00:31:17.289 --> 00:31:20.889
I certainly had lots of passion but very little focus and I

00:31:20.909 --> 00:31:21.909
hope you all can

00:31:21.909 --> 00:31:23.669
find that area of passion for you.

00:31:23.689 --> 00:31:27.589
What is that issue, that problem, that thing in society that

00:31:27.609 --> 00:31:29.029
you look out and

00:31:29.049 --> 00:31:32.049
upsets you or you think could be done better or could be

00:31:30.859 --> 00:31:31.859
fixed?

00:31:31.859 --> 00:31:33.829
What's your holy discontent?

00:31:33.849 --> 00:31:36.849
And that might be your first clue as to what your calling

00:31:36.849 --> 00:31:37.849
is.

00:31:37.849 --> 00:31:41.490
Frederick Beekner, theologian, says, "Calling is where the

00:31:41.509 --> 00:31:42.740
deep gladness of your

00:31:42.759 --> 00:31:48.039
heart meets the world's great need."

00:31:48.059 --> 00:31:52.500
And I think it's worth -- take some reflection to put those

00:31:52.519 --> 00:31:53.879
together but if you

00:31:53.899 --> 00:31:57.149
can, then you'll be ready when the right phone rings or when

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:58.000
that right call

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.470
comes.

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:01.740
So what are your dreams?

00:32:01.589 --> 00:32:04.079
What problems do you wanna solve?

00:32:04.099 --> 00:32:07.099
What price are you willing to pay to, uh, to make that

00:32:06.289 --> 00:32:07.289
happen?

00:32:07.289 --> 00:32:10.659
So we close and I hope to turn into a conversation.

00:32:10.679 --> 00:32:14.599
I just wanna share my favorite blessing from the Franciscan

00:32:14.619 --> 00:32:16.549
tradition.

00:32:16.569 --> 00:32:19.769
"May God bless you with discomfort at easy answers, half

00:32:19.789 --> 00:32:21.599
truths, and superficial

00:32:21.619 --> 00:32:26.189
relationships so that you may live deeply within your heart.

00:32:26.209 --> 00:32:30.500
May God bless you with anger at injustice, oppression, and

00:32:30.519 --> 00:32:31.519
exploitation of

00:32:31.519 --> 00:32:36.439
people so that you may wish for justice, freedom, and peace.

00:32:36.459 --> 00:32:39.209
And may God bless you enough foolishness to belief that you

00:32:39.139 --> 00:32:40.139
can make a

00:32:40.139 --> 00:32:43.409
difference in this world so that you can do what others say

00:32:43.429 --> 00:32:44.429
cannot be done."

00:32:44.429 --> 00:32:46.179
It's great to be with you.

00:32:45.159 --> 00:32:46.159
Thank you.

00:32:46.159 --> 00:32:46.359
&gt;&gt;

00:32:46.159 --> 00:32:47.159
[Clapping]

00:32:46.159 --> 00:33:07.869
Reckford: I think we have time for some questions and I

00:33:07.889 --> 00:33:08.889
think they asked if

00:33:08.889 --> 00:33:12.379
people would wait for the mic to be passed down so they can

00:33:12.399 --> 00:33:13.399
capture them so

00:33:13.399 --> 00:33:14.399
everybody can hear.

00:33:14.149 --> 00:33:14.349
&gt;&gt;

00:33:14.149 --> 00:33:15.149
[Quiet]

00:33:14.149 --> 00:33:18.829
Audience: Well, thank you for coming.

00:33:18.849 --> 00:33:21.849
I wanted to ask a question going back to the sale of

00:33:21.849 --> 00:33:23.039
Musicland.

00:33:23.059 --> 00:33:25.629
Seems like you had some constituents there with competing

00:33:25.649 --> 00:33:26.649
interests between

00:33:26.649 --> 00:33:30.099
shareholders and your team and maybe even customers and I

00:33:30.119 --> 00:33:31.529
was curious how your

00:33:31.549 --> 00:33:34.649
faith and how Christian principles formed how you balanced

00:33:34.669 --> 00:33:35.909
those interests, and

00:33:35.929 --> 00:33:39.059
in particular, was it difficult for you to reconcile the

00:33:39.079 --> 00:33:40.849
sale of an asset that

00:33:40.869 --> 00:33:43.779
it seems like you had some suspicions was a failing asset

00:33:43.799 --> 00:33:44.799
and maybe the sale

00:33:44.799 --> 00:33:45.799
wouldn't go well?

00:33:45.449 --> 00:33:49.119
Reckford: You know, I think -- the interesting thing was

00:33:49.139 --> 00:33:50.389
that Best Buy had a

00:33:50.389 --> 00:33:53.899
hypothesis that turned out to be flawed but they had a model

00:33:53.919 --> 00:33:54.919
of using the real

00:33:54.919 --> 00:33:58.099
estate in a huge number of relationships with had with teens

00:33:58.119 --> 00:33:59.779
in malls to

00:33:59.799 --> 00:34:03.589
leverage those for electronics sales and so it was a

00:34:03.609 --> 00:34:04.909
plausible strategy of why

00:34:04.929 --> 00:34:07.429
it was gonna be different than -- it was gonna migrate

00:34:07.139 --> 00:34:09.759
around music, umm, and

00:34:09.779 --> 00:34:10.779
didn't work.

00:34:10.279 --> 00:34:13.199
But we -- I think were more -- they were way more optimistic

00:34:13.219 --> 00:34:14.219
and we were more

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:17.779
optimistic at the time, umm, that that was actually the best

00:34:17.799 --> 00:34:18.949
path because

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:22.210
otherwise we could see sort of a long eroding tale where we'

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:23.230
d just be closing

00:34:23.239 --> 00:34:26.809
stores as leases came up because retail, you know, the

00:34:26.829 --> 00:34:27.900
leverage is so strong

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:30.789
that relatively small decreases in sales wipe out all your

00:34:30.809 --> 00:34:31.809
profitability very

00:34:31.809 --> 00:34:36.099
quickly, uh, so it's a very thin business.

00:34:36.119 --> 00:34:40.519
So it -- but I think it is, you know, much easier to look

00:34:40.539 --> 00:34:41.789
back now with all the

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:44.469
data than -- than when you're looking forward but Best Buy

00:34:44.489 --> 00:34:45.739
was on a roll and

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:48.279
they had the financial strength to underwrite the sort of

00:34:48.299 --> 00:34:49.299
revamping of the

00:34:49.299 --> 00:34:51.799
stores that we didn't have the capital to do.

00:34:51.279 --> 00:34:54.920
So it struck us at the time as sort of the best option for

00:34:54.940 --> 00:34:55.940
everybody. Everybody

00:34:55.940 --> 00:34:59.369
should go through a merger.

00:34:59.389 --> 00:35:03.539
Being acquired once, it's an incredible education.

00:35:03.559 --> 00:35:06.769
I think the danger of -- of almost every acquisition is,

00:35:06.789 --> 00:35:08.039
even if they're done

00:35:07.409 --> 00:35:09.909
for strategic purposes and you acquire because you want the

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:10.500
skills and gifts of

00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:13.500
the group you're acquiring, there's an acquirer's bias that

00:35:13.429 --> 00:35:14.679
I've seen whether I

00:35:14.639 --> 00:35:17.389
was in the investment banking side or on the operating side

00:35:17.079 --> 00:35:18.079
where the acquirer

00:35:18.079 --> 00:35:21.900
tends to think they have always answers so they, uh, I think

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:23.369
Best Buy made some

00:35:23.389 --> 00:35:26.480
major strategic mistakes by not fully understanding what,

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:27.500
uh, the assets they

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:30.059
were buying before they put all the changes in place.

00:35:30.079 --> 00:35:33.710
But it's -- I think it's a -- it's a constant juggling act

00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:34.730
of all those stake

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:35.730
holders.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:35.179
&gt;&gt;

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:35.980
[Quiet]

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:50.599
Audience: Umm, well, thanks also for coming.

00:35:50.619 --> 00:35:55.579
I think a lot of times ministry organizations have a lot of

00:35:55.599 --> 00:35:56.599
difficulty in the

00:35:56.599 --> 00:35:59.469
management of people, how they hire, how they fire, how they

00:35:59.489 --> 00:36:00.929
grow.

00:36:00.949 --> 00:36:06.110
Many times they can't fire just out of obligation or out of,

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:07.130
I don't know,

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:09.880
some -- can you just kinda talk about how you've managed

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:10.880
Habitat if you've seen

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:14.110
some of those issues when you come in and how you're gonna

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:15.019
grow and build the

00:36:15.039 --> 00:36:16.039
business go forward?

00:36:15.789 --> 00:36:17.039
Reckford: Thank you.

00:36:17.059 --> 00:36:21.230
It's -- it's really interesting challenge I think and

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:22.250
actually it was so --

00:36:22.250 --> 00:36:25.750
that's part of why it was so valuable to work in a church

00:36:25.119 --> 00:36:26.119
environment before

00:36:26.119 --> 00:36:27.130
coming to Habitat.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:29.650
If you want the most complex management challenge in some

00:36:29.539 --> 00:36:30.789
ways try to manage a

00:36:30.429 --> 00:36:33.569
synagogue, or a mosque, or a church because you've got

00:36:33.589 --> 00:36:34.920
immense ownership, all

00:36:34.940 --> 00:36:37.699
volunteers, very little leverage over the people doing all

00:36:37.719 --> 00:36:39.719
the work and, uh, and

00:36:39.739 --> 00:36:41.739
an enormous shared ownership of the mission.

00:36:41.759 --> 00:36:44.730
So they're great assets but great complexity as well.

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:48.569
What I found is, umm, that was really helpful to learn is

00:36:48.589 --> 00:36:49.589
you can manage

00:36:49.589 --> 00:36:52.339
volunteers and if you can manage volunteers you can -- it's

00:36:51.829 --> 00:36:52.829
much easier to

00:36:52.829 --> 00:36:55.980
manage people but, umm, we learned that you can hold

00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:57.000
volunteers accountable,

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:00.679
that you, uh, that actually you need to do the same work to

00:37:00.699 --> 00:37:01.699
make sure that a

00:37:01.699 --> 00:37:05.639
volunteer is in a role that he or she values and uses their

00:37:05.659 --> 00:37:06.659
gifts well.

00:37:06.659 --> 00:37:09.659
One of the things that faith groups do poorly is often they'

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:10.440
ll put very high

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:13.190
capacity people and put them in the wrong roles and Habitat

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:14.190
was doing the same

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:15.190
thing.

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:17.940
And so, uh, the second thing I learned is that you're not,

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:19.469
umm, you're not doing

00:37:19.489 --> 00:37:22.489
someone a favor by letting them sit in the wrong role not

00:37:22.109 --> 00:37:23.109
being successful.

00:37:23.109 --> 00:37:25.859
That's bad for the mission and actually bad for the

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:25.980
individual.

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:29.819
And so, uh, I was sharing with a group last night, you know,

00:37:29.839 --> 00:37:31.089
I think what I had

00:37:31.089 --> 00:37:35.319
to learn was, umm, that it has to be integrated.

00:37:35.339 --> 00:37:39.299
I got pushed, you know, often as we put in change and sort

00:37:39.319 --> 00:37:40.389
of raise

00:37:40.409 --> 00:37:43.929
accountability, that got thrown back as, well, you're not

00:37:43.949 --> 00:37:45.009
being faithful anymore

00:37:45.029 --> 00:37:48.179
and sort of either we were gonna be grass root and is

00:37:48.199 --> 00:37:49.449
faithful or we were gonna

00:37:49.449 --> 00:37:51.949
be professional and that was termed as "going corporate" and

00:37:51.949 --> 00:37:52.949
that was viewed as

00:37:52.949 --> 00:37:53.949
a bad thing.

00:37:53.699 --> 00:37:54.949
And the response was, no.

00:37:54.949 --> 00:37:58.449
We're using other people's money to do God's work to serve

00:37:57.319 --> 00:37:58.319
the poor of the

00:37:58.319 --> 00:37:59.319
world.

00:37:58.569 --> 00:38:01.069
We should have much higher standards than the private sector

00:38:00.369 --> 00:38:01.369
because the

00:38:01.369 --> 00:38:02.369
standards are so high.

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:06.730
So we're gonna be unapologetic about accountability and much

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:07.750
of the practice we

00:38:07.750 --> 00:38:10.900
do if we standard any well-run organization I hope in terms

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:11.920
of making sure

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:15.799
everyone has very clear roles, very clear responsibilities,

00:38:15.819 --> 00:38:16.920
they know what

00:38:16.940 --> 00:38:20.639
they're supposed to be accountable for, then we reward them

00:38:20.659 --> 00:38:22.219
for doing their

00:38:22.239 --> 00:38:24.170
work.

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:27.440
It's, umm, I think it's hard, though, because there's that

00:38:26.319 --> 00:38:27.319
whole being nice.

00:38:27.319 --> 00:38:29.339
You know, anybody here from Minnesota?

00:38:29.359 --> 00:38:32.469
So there's, you know, they call Minnesota nice so the land

00:38:32.489 --> 00:38:33.489
of -- the land of

00:38:33.489 --> 00:38:38.829
Lutherans and woe begotten where nobody wants to say the

00:38:38.849 --> 00:38:40.539
hard things and I think

00:38:40.559 --> 00:38:44.989
part of, uh, my view is, it's not -- again, you don't have

00:38:45.009 --> 00:38:46.259
to be nice or, you

00:38:46.259 --> 00:38:50.139
know, bad cop Antichrist model, that there's a -- that you

00:38:50.159 --> 00:38:51.509
can be nice and --

00:38:51.529 --> 00:38:54.440
and be really clear and transparent and sometimes there's a

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:55.460
-- the other thing

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.380
we see in nonprofits is -- the thing is in nonprofit

00:38:58.400 --> 00:38:59.650
management is you get two

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:00.400
groups in nonprofits.

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:03.360
One group who are people with world class skills and gifts

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:04.380
and talents and who

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:07.380
are there because of the mission and I think there's another

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:07.630
group, umm,

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:11.110
sometimes who are there, you know, somewhat as refugees or

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:12.309
to be rescued rather

00:39:12.329 --> 00:39:15.929
than -- rather than to, uh, to make a difference and that's

00:39:15.949 --> 00:39:16.949
really hard.

00:39:16.949 --> 00:39:20.949
And it is, umm -- what we found is we wanna treat people

00:39:20.969 --> 00:39:21.969
respectfully, we wanna

00:39:21.969 --> 00:39:25.739
treat people well, up, but at the end of the day, the first

00:39:25.759 --> 00:39:26.759
thing is the mission

00:39:26.759 --> 00:39:30.009
and what we can't do is allow somebody who's not performing

00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:30.380
well because if --

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:33.630
if you send a signal that it's okay not to perform well,

00:39:33.500 --> 00:39:34.769
that starts sort of

00:39:34.789 --> 00:39:37.549
demotivating your high performing folks and pulling them

00:39:37.569 --> 00:39:38.569
down.

00:39:38.569 --> 00:39:39.819
But it's not easy.

00:39:39.039 --> 00:39:40.039
Audience: Hi.

00:39:40.039 --> 00:39:42.429
Thanks.

00:39:42.449 --> 00:39:45.449
One of the things I've been most impressed about is your

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:45.339
ability to scale over

00:39:45.359 --> 00:39:46.359
the last five years.

00:39:46.359 --> 00:39:49.359
Can you talk about what are some of the levers that you

00:39:49.079 --> 00:39:50.579
pulled to be able to do

00:39:50.579 --> 00:39:53.329
that in the nonprofit world and what are some of the

00:39:53.329 --> 00:39:54.329
challenges you face going

00:39:54.329 --> 00:39:57.079
forward as you look at taking it to the next level?

00:39:56.829 --> 00:39:57.829
Reckford: Thank you.

00:39:57.829 --> 00:40:00.329
It's, umm, you know, some things -- true, every organization

00:39:59.989 --> 00:40:00.989
the best ideas are

00:40:00.989 --> 00:40:04.239
all at the field level so we certainly didn't come in and

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:05.130
say, here are all the

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:07.630
great new ideas. We actually -- I traveled around the world

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:09.000
and just looked for

00:40:09.019 --> 00:40:12.809
what was -- what was working or what were all the things

00:40:12.829 --> 00:40:13.829
people were secretly

00:40:13.829 --> 00:40:16.579
doing but didn't -- you know, weren't saying because they

00:40:15.829 --> 00:40:16.829
didn't think that

00:40:16.829 --> 00:40:19.579
would be okay back at headquarters and we tried to get

00:40:19.579 --> 00:40:20.809
permission to -- and a

00:40:20.829 --> 00:40:22.940
couple things particularly drove growth.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:26.880
One, right as I started, Katrina hit, which was not on my

00:40:26.900 --> 00:40:28.309
hundred day plan but

00:40:28.329 --> 00:40:32.980
it freed us up to break a lot of rules in terms of doing

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:34.000
something -- and we

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:37.449
needed to do something big by our standards in the Gulf so

00:40:37.469 --> 00:40:38.719
it forced us to sit

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:41.690
down and sort of reassess the model of how can we do enough

00:40:41.549 --> 00:40:42.659
to matter in that --

00:40:42.679 --> 00:40:43.679
in that?

00:40:43.659 --> 00:40:47.089
So we got the board to formally bless disaster recovery as a

00:40:47.109 --> 00:40:48.109
formal part of our

00:40:48.109 --> 00:40:50.599
work which had been informally there for a wile.

00:40:50.619 --> 00:40:54.360
And we used the tsunami in Asia and Katrina in the U.S. as

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:55.719
models of scaling up.

00:40:55.739 --> 00:40:58.739
We ended up helping 23,000 families in the tsunami which is

00:40:58.630 --> 00:40:59.630
bigger than anything

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:02.880
we had ever done and we were the leading -- we may still be

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:03.630
the leading home

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:06.130
builder in the -- in the Gulf Coast since Katrina which,

00:41:06.130 --> 00:41:07.380
again, is not a typical

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:08.130
role for Habitat.

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:11.460
But in doing that, we used that to learn how to move faster

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:12.659
and to do whole

00:41:12.679 --> 00:41:15.869
community development and operated to scale and operate in

00:41:15.889 --> 00:41:16.889
partnerships in way

00:41:16.889 --> 00:41:18.389
that is we hadn't before.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:21.250
So now Haiti and Chile, we're doing -- we're gonna try to

00:41:21.019 --> 00:41:22.259
help 50,000 families

00:41:22.279 --> 00:41:26.559
in Haiti and a similarly large number we hope in Chile over

00:41:26.579 --> 00:41:28.539
time.

00:41:28.559 --> 00:41:29.969
So disaster response was big.

00:41:29.989 --> 00:41:33.309
The second one was housing micro finance, uh, and micro

00:41:33.329 --> 00:41:34.500
finance extremely well

00:41:34.519 --> 00:41:39.119
established but almost -- almost 90 to 95 percent of the

00:41:39.139 --> 00:41:40.139
people in the world

00:41:40.139 --> 00:41:43.889
have no access to a home loan of any kind and so what we're

00:41:43.369 --> 00:41:44.369
-- in addition to

00:41:44.369 --> 00:41:47.480
all the -- so we do housing lending out of necessity but it'

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:48.500
s not our

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:49.500
preference.

00:41:48.769 --> 00:41:52.019
So one of our goals is to get the micro finance world to

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:52.659
begin lending for home

00:41:52.679 --> 00:41:55.539
improvement loans to families that have been taking out

00:41:55.559 --> 00:41:56.559
successful small

00:41:56.559 --> 00:42:00.369
business loans and we're, umm, that's been a -- we see that

00:42:00.389 --> 00:42:01.639
as a huge next scale

00:42:00.889 --> 00:42:01.889
up.

00:42:01.889 --> 00:42:04.889
We're launching a global housing micro finance fund to be a

00:42:04.099 --> 00:42:05.110
wholesale lender to

00:42:05.130 --> 00:42:08.130
MFIs and then we'll technical assistance if they'll do the

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:08.630
lending.

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:12.130
And that's -- we see that as one of our next big bridges for

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:13.150
scale.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:16.469
Uh, and then the third one would be around advocacy, umm,

00:42:16.489 --> 00:42:18.110
trying to actually

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:21.789
change policies so we can impact way more people than the --

00:42:21.809 --> 00:42:22.809
than the houses we

00:42:22.809 --> 00:42:25.559
build and our big focus is on secure tenure and property

00:42:25.309 --> 00:42:26.309
rights especially for

00:42:26.309 --> 00:42:28.059
women in developing countries.

00:42:28.079 --> 00:42:32.549
So we're trying to change rules to, uh, create protections

00:42:32.569 --> 00:42:33.569
and, again, if we

00:42:33.569 --> 00:42:35.509
can, use crises to do that.

00:42:35.529 --> 00:42:38.589
So we're doing big projects around HIV, widows and orphan

00:42:38.609 --> 00:42:39.900
programs in Africa,

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:42.039
particularly in southeast Asia.

00:42:42.059 --> 00:42:45.059
In India and the tsunami, we used the -- so the chance to

00:42:44.909 --> 00:42:46.159
come in and serve we

00:42:46.059 --> 00:42:48.730
titled all the homes along the southeast Indian coast and

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:50.000
women's names only and

00:42:49.719 --> 00:42:51.980
got the other nonprofits to do the same thing.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:54.599
That actually had a pretty profound social change in terms

00:42:54.619 --> 00:42:56.319
of -- of changing,

00:42:56.339 --> 00:42:58.039
uh, changing the values there.

00:42:58.059 --> 00:43:02.230
So -- and similarly, in Africa in a lot of countries if a

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:03.250
woman's husband dies,

00:43:03.250 --> 00:43:06.319
his husband inherits the property and the women and children

00:43:06.339 --> 00:43:07.809
are left out.

00:43:07.829 --> 00:43:10.829
So we're trying to get laws changed to allow joint titling

00:43:10.719 --> 00:43:12.699
and so we see the --

00:43:12.719 --> 00:43:16.210
the ability to allow families to stay in communities as a

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:17.480
way we can scale way

00:43:17.449 --> 00:43:20.309
beyond our -- our core building.

00:43:20.329 --> 00:43:21.889
That's a few of 'em.

00:43:21.909 --> 00:43:22.109
&gt;&gt;

00:43:22.109 --> 00:43:23.109
[Quiet]

00:43:22.109 --> 00:43:32.670
Audience: Thank you very much for coming.

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:35.190
Umm, I worked in the nonprofit sector before coming to

00:43:35.139 --> 00:43:36.139
business school and was

00:43:36.139 --> 00:43:40.269
given the advice by somebody, umm, excuse me, at the

00:43:40.289 --> 00:43:41.289
millennium challenge

00:43:41.289 --> 00:43:44.039
corporation who had gone directly from business school back

00:43:44.059 --> 00:43:45.940
to the NCC that had

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:48.960
she -- to do it over again, she really would have gone to

00:43:48.099 --> 00:43:49.099
the private sector

00:43:49.099 --> 00:43:51.589
before going to the nonprofit sector.

00:43:51.609 --> 00:43:54.609
And I'm wondering if you can speak a little bit about --

00:43:54.129 --> 00:43:55.170
although your intention

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:58.799
was clear to go to -- to, umm, Goldman Sachs, what you

00:43:58.819 --> 00:44:00.069
learned in all of those

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:02.710
years in the private sector that has made you more effective

00:44:02.279 --> 00:44:03.279
if your current

00:44:03.279 --> 00:44:04.279
role.

00:44:03.529 --> 00:44:06.029
Reckford: You know, I -- it's a really good question.

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:09.289
We were actually talking about it right before this session.

00:44:09.309 --> 00:44:12.059
I don't think there's a right or wrong rule.

00:44:11.609 --> 00:44:14.389
Umm, I think there are enormous benefits to getting private

00:44:14.409 --> 00:44:15.480
sector experience

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:18.670
and I think there are -- if you were to generalize and I

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:19.690
think there are

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:22.569
brilliantly run nonprofits that are better than -- than most

00:44:22.589 --> 00:44:23.589
companies and then

00:44:23.589 --> 00:44:26.589
there are a lot of poorly-run -- awful lot of poorly-run

00:44:26.009 --> 00:44:27.009
nonprofits that are out

00:44:27.009 --> 00:44:30.009
there likewise there are a lot of well-run companies and not

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:30.170
so well-run

00:44:30.170 --> 00:44:31.170
companies.

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:31.949
So you need to choose carefully.

00:44:31.969 --> 00:44:36.259
I think for me my hypothesis was I would get more experience

00:44:36.279 --> 00:44:37.279
faster in the

00:44:37.279 --> 00:44:39.279
business world then could take that with me.

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:42.750
I think what I would temper that with is a means to an end.

00:44:42.609 --> 00:44:46.359
I wouldn't take a job that you really don't wanna do just so

00:44:45.859 --> 00:44:46.859
you can get to

00:44:46.859 --> 00:44:49.359
something else 'cause I think the chance of being successful

00:44:49.109 --> 00:44:50.109
aren't as good.

00:44:50.109 --> 00:44:52.109
And I think there are great individual opportunities.

00:44:52.109 --> 00:44:54.359
If you take an organization -- take something Muhammad Yunus

00:44:54.359 --> 00:44:55.359
or Fazle Abed and

00:44:55.359 --> 00:44:59.319
BRAC-- you know, if you could work with a leader like that,

00:44:59.339 --> 00:45:00.619
that would dwarf any

00:45:00.639 --> 00:45:03.579
business leader you could ever find in terms of staggering

00:45:03.599 --> 00:45:04.599
entrepreneurial

00:45:04.599 --> 00:45:05.769
talent.

00:45:05.789 --> 00:45:08.789
But those are -- but you have to find the right leader to

00:45:08.539 --> 00:45:09.539
attach yourself to.

00:45:09.539 --> 00:45:13.159
So I think it's -- it's my view is I would explore both

00:45:13.179 --> 00:45:14.179
paths if your passions

00:45:14.179 --> 00:45:17.380
are leading you that way and then pick, umm, you know, the

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:18.400
place you think

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:21.150
you're gonna get the highest learning curve and -- and the

00:45:21.009 --> 00:45:22.009
most tangible

00:45:22.009 --> 00:45:24.190
experience in the -- in the shorter term.

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:27.710
And then you've got a way in sort of the golden hand cuffs

00:45:27.339 --> 00:45:28.880
trap of once -- a lot

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:32.150
of people go in saying they're gonna do this and then they

00:45:31.739 --> 00:45:32.739
never actually make

00:45:32.739 --> 00:45:36.179
the move and 25 years later they have all these regrets and

00:45:36.199 --> 00:45:37.199
they weren't that

00:45:37.199 --> 00:45:38.199
happy or fulfilled.

00:45:38.210 --> 00:45:41.510
So it's a -- I think you have to be careful.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:41.730
&gt;&gt;

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:42.730
[Quiet]

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:43.980
Audience: Do we have time for one more question?

